Install a theme, import it with the CLI, and wire the generated module once. For the full guide — available themes, integration themes, dark mode, nested themes, and production builds — see {@link generic:use-a-theme}.

```
import {Theme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {themes, defaultThemeSlug} from './astryx-themes';

function App() {
  return (
    <Theme theme={themes[defaultThemeSlug]}>
      <YourApp />
    </Theme>
  );
}
```

Use `defineTheme` with token overrides, scale configs, and component style overrides. For the full guide — palette generation, extending themes, adaptations, custom variants, and production builds — see {@link generic:author-a-theme}.

```
{@command theme add stone}   # use an existing theme
{@command theme eject stone} # fork its source to customize
{@command theme build src/themes/stone/stoneTheme.ts}
```

For a new coordinated color system, run `{@command theme palette generate palette.config.json --out palette.generated.ts}`. Review and commit the generated palette module and receipt, import its `palette` export, then reference stable stops from semantic tokens. Regenerating an accepted palette updates every mapped token that keeps those references; generation does not rewrite the theme.

```
import {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {palette} from './palette.generated';

const myTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'my-theme',
  tokens: {
    '--color-background-surface': [
      palette.neutral.light[100],
      palette.neutral.dark[15],
    ],
  },
});
```

A theme sets the dimension of each Icon size in its stylesheet: `width` and `height` on `components.icon['size:sm']` (or `xsm`, `md`, `lg`) resize that size's glyph, for semantic icon names and SVG components passed to `Icon` alike. Give the dimension in `px` or `rem`: a semantic icon takes its font size from it, so an `em` value would also grow its box. A size the theme leaves alone stays 12, 16, 20, or 24px at a 16px root (`xsm`, `sm`, `md`, `lg`) and scales with the root font size; `rem` keeps that scaling. The four size names are fixed.

```
const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  components: {
    icon: {
      'size:sm': {width: '1.125rem', height: '1.125rem'},
      'size:lg': {width: '1.75rem', height: '1.75rem'},
    },
  },
});
```

Upgrade every Astryx package your app lists in one command, to the same release. Each theme names the exact Core release it works with, so a theme left on an older release no longer matches the new Core.

```
{@pm install @astryxdesign/core@latest @astryxdesign/theme-neutral@latest @astryxdesign/cli@latest}
{@command upgrade --from <old Core version> --apply}
```

Add every other Astryx package your app lists to the same command, and pass the Core version you had before to `--from`. If your package manager holds new releases back, exempt the whole `@astryxdesign/*` scope; in pnpm, `minimumReleaseAgeExclude: ['@astryxdesign/*']`. Exempting only some of its packages installs packages from different releases side by side.

Import the reset and base styles in your global CSS. Then run `{@command theme add neutral}`; the generated app module imports the theme’s production CSS and font CSS.

```
@import '@astryxdesign/core/reset.css';
@import '@astryxdesign/core/astryx.css';
```

Run `{@command theme list}` to see every theme.

```
import {Theme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {themes, defaultThemeSlug} from './astryx-themes';

<Theme theme={themes[defaultThemeSlug]}><App /></Theme>
```

- `@astryxdesign/theme-neutral`: muted and minimal; a good starting point
- `@astryxdesign/theme-butter`: warm, golden tones with blue accents
- `@astryxdesign/theme-chocolate`: rich chocolate and caramel tones
- `@astryxdesign/theme-gothic`: dark-only theme with ink and noir influences
- `@astryxdesign/theme-matcha`: earthy greens and botanical tones
- `@astryxdesign/theme-stone`: warm neutrals inspired by sandstone
- `@astryxdesign/theme-y2k`: playful early-2000s pop aesthetic

These stylesheets are cascade-layered: the reset loads in @layer reset and component styles in @layer astryx-base. If your project has existing global CSS, a legacy reset, or Tailwind, declare the layer order explicitly and assign every stylesheet to a layer deliberately: unlayered styles and later layers both override astryx-base regardless of specificity. Before building screens, read the two cascade layer sections of {@link generic:migration-setup}.

Run {@link generic:use-a-theme} for the full theming guide.

Import each component from its own entry, the path `{@command component <Name>}` prints. This keeps bundles small and makes intent clear.

```
import {Button} from '@astryxdesign/core/Button';
import {VStack} from '@astryxdesign/core/VStack';

export default function Page() {
  return (
    <VStack gap={2}>
      <Button label="Hello Astryx" onClick={() => alert('Hi!')} />
    </VStack>
  );
}
```

Astryx components support various styling solutions, from plain CSS and `className` to Tailwind and CSS-in-JS. See {@link namespace:styling} for the full guide. Astryx also has a deep integration with [StyleX](https://stylexjs.com/), an atomic CSS-in-JS library: create styles with `stylex.create()` and pass them to components with the `xstyle` prop.

```
import * as stylex from '@stylexjs/stylex';

const overrides = stylex.create({
  save: { alignSelf: 'flex-end', marginTop: 16 },
});

<Button label="Save" xstyle={overrides.save} />
```

Import the typed color tokens and use them in `stylex.create()`; they resolve to the active theme and color mode.

```
import * as stylex from '@stylexjs/stylex';
import {colorVars} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme/tokens.stylex';

const styles = stylex.create({
  container: {
    backgroundColor: colorVars['--color-background-surface'],
    color: colorVars['--color-text-primary'],
    borderColor: colorVars['--color-border'],
  },
  accent: {
    color: colorVars['--color-text-accent'],
  },
});
```

Stack, HStack, VStack, Grid, Toolbar, OverflowList, and ChatMessageList take a `gap` step: 0, 0.5, 1, 1.5, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, or 10 (×4px). Carousel takes the steps from 0 to 4. For custom layouts, use the spacing tokens in StyleX.

```
// Via component props (preferred)
<Stack gap={4}>{/* 16px gap */}</Stack>

// Via StyleX tokens (custom layouts)
import {spacingVars} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme/tokens.stylex';

const styles = stylex.create({
  custom: {
    padding: spacingVars['--spacing-4'],
    gap: spacingVars['--spacing-3'],
  },
});
```

Configurable surfaces expose a single `elevation` prop instead of asking consumers to hand-write a box-shadow. It takes the graded enum `none | low | med | high`, narrowed per component to the steps that surface needs: Card, ClickableCard, SelectableCard, Button, IconButton, ButtonGroup, ToggleButton, and Banner expose the full scale, while ChatComposer exposes only `none | low`. `none` is a flat literal (`box-shadow: none`); the other levels map to the `--shadow-*` tokens above, so a surface stays theme-agnostic.

Prop defaults preserve current appearance: every surface defaults to `none` except ChatComposer, which defaults to `low` to keep its raised look. Set `elevation="none"` to flatten it; the flat composer draws a border with the same rest / hover / focus treatment as a text input.

```
import {Plus} from 'lucide-react';

// Flat by default; raise only when the surface needs to float.
<Card elevation="low">Raised card</Card>

// A floating action button.
<IconButton icon={<Icon icon={Plus} />} label="New" variant="primary" elevation="med" />

// Flatten the composer (defaults to 'low').
<ChatComposer elevation="none" onSubmit={handleSubmit} />
```

Always float; no prop: intrinsic overlays (Dialog, Popover, Tooltip, Toast, HoverCard, DropdownMenu, and the components that compose them) bake their elevation in and never expose the prop. Flow content (inputs, Text, layout) is never elevated; input fields use inset rings, which are a separate concept from elevation.

When building a custom surface, read elevation from the token scale rather than a hand-rolled shadow; the same tokens the elevation prop resolves to.

```
import {shadowVars} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme/tokens.stylex';

const styles = stylex.create({
  dropdown: {
    boxShadow: shadowVars['--shadow-med'],
  },
  dialog: {
    boxShadow: shadowVars['--shadow-high'],
  },
  inputFocused: {
    boxShadow: shadowVars['--shadow-inset-selected'],
  },
});
```

Inside a padded, rounded container, give nested elements a radius of outer radius minus padding so the curves stay concentric. Card does not do this for your content.

```
.inner {
  border-radius: max(0px, calc(var(--radius-container) - var(--spacing-4)));
}
```

Set transition durations and easing from the motion tokens, so a theme that retunes motion retunes your animations too. Ease something arriving on `--ease-entrance` and something moving on screen on `--ease-move`, so it follows the theme's `character`, and something leaving on `--ease-exit`, which stays the same at every `character`.

```
import {durationVars, easeVars} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme/tokens.stylex';

const styles = stylex.create({
  fadeIn: {
    transitionProperty: 'opacity',
    transitionDuration: durationVars['--duration-fast'],
    transitionTimingFunction: easeVars['--ease-standard'],
  },
  slideUp: {
    transitionProperty: 'transform, opacity',
    transitionDuration: durationVars['--duration-medium'],
    transitionTimingFunction: easeVars['--ease-entrance'],
  },
});
```

```
{@pm install @astryxdesign/core @stylexjs/stylex @astryxdesign/theme-neutral}
```

```
@import "@astryxdesign/core/reset.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/core/astryx.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/theme-neutral/theme.css";
```

Done. Components work out of the box — style them with `className`, `xstyle`, or `style`. Swap `theme-neutral` for any theme package (`{@command theme list}` shows what's available).

```
{@pm install @astryxdesign/core @stylexjs/stylex @astryxdesign/theme-neutral}
```

```
@layer reset, theme, base, astryx-base, astryx-theme, components, utilities;

@import "tailwindcss/theme.css" layer(theme);
@import "tailwindcss/preflight.css" layer(base);
@import "@astryxdesign/core/reset.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/core/astryx.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/theme-neutral/theme.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/core/tailwind-theme.css";
@import "tailwindcss/utilities.css" layer(utilities);
```

The `@layer` declaration is critical — without it, component styles outrank Tailwind utilities and `className` overrides stop working. The bridge (`tailwind-theme.css`) maps classes like `text-primary`, `bg-surface`, and `rounded-lg` to design tokens with no JS.

A project with Tailwind already set up needs a smaller change, which {@link generic:tailwind} shows. For Tailwind v3, see the layer workaround in {@link generic:migration-setup}.

Only needed when you write your own StyleX (`stylex.create()`) or swizzle a component (`{@command swizzle <Component>}`), which copies its StyleX source into your app. The plain path already supports `xstyle` overrides — this path is for your own StyleX code. Some built-in page templates also use StyleX for layout; if you scaffold one and see "Unexpected stylex.create call at runtime", add the compiler plugin for your bundler below.

```
{@pm install @astryxdesign/core @stylexjs/stylex @astryxdesign/theme-neutral}
```

```
@import "@astryxdesign/core/reset.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/core/astryx.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/theme-neutral/theme.css";
```

Keep `astryx.css`: Astryx component styles always come from the prebuilt stylesheets. Point the StyleX compiler at your own source only, never at `node_modules/@astryxdesign`. Add the plugin for your bundler:

If a swizzled component fails at runtime with a `stylex.create` error, the app is missing a StyleX compiler: see the StyleX Build Setup section of {@link generic:tokens-and-setup}.

`{@command swizzle <Component>}` writes an `astryx-swizzle.json` marker into an output folder it owns (one the copy creates, an empty one, or one it marked before); every module under that folder then compiles as Astryx source, so keep copies in a folder of their own, such as the default `components/astryx`. For a folder that already holds other files it writes no marker and says how to add one by hand. With `@astryxdesign/build`'s Babel, PostCSS and Vite integrations, or `withAstryx()` under webpack, a copy under that folder compiles into `astryx-base`, below `astryx-theme`, so theme component overrides reach it as they reach the package component. Under Turbopack, and with the stock StyleX plugins above, a copy's rules land in your own styles, above `astryx-theme`, and theme component overrides do not reach it.

Every Astryx package whose components carry styles ships its own prebuilt stylesheet. Import it after `astryx.css` for each package you use. All of them place their rules in the same `astryx-base` layer, so your theme overrides win over every package.

```
@import "@astryxdesign/core/reset.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/core/astryx.css";
@import "@astryxdesign/richtext/richtext.css"; /* Rich Text */
@import "@astryxdesign/lab/lab.css"; /* Lab */
@import "@astryxdesign/charts/charts.css"; /* Charts */
@import "@astryxdesign/theme-neutral/theme.css";
```

Themes can replace the default SVGs for any built-in meaning with the `icons` field in `defineTheme()`. This lets you swap the icon set (e.g. heroicons → lucide) without touching component code, and keeps lookup scoped to the active theme instead of mutating global defaults.

```
import {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {XMarkIcon, ChevronDownIcon} from '@heroicons/react/24/outline';

export const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  icons: {
    close: <XMarkIcon />,
    chevronDown: <ChevronDownIcon />,
  },
});
```

An entry can also be a function of the request, so the theme maps Icon's size to its icon library's own props or artwork. `Icon` calls it with `{size, variant}`, where `size` is the size it renders at (`xsm`, `sm`, `md`, or `lg`: an explicit `size`, else the owning component's default, else `md`). Reads that return one node — `getIcon()`, `useIcon()`, `getIconRegistry()`, and the theme's `icons` field — call it with `{size: 'md'}`. The same entry shape works in `registerIcons()`.

```
import {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {Info} from 'lucide-react';

export const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  icons: {
    // A heavier stroke keeps the small size legible.
    info: ({size}) => <Info strokeWidth={size === 'sm' || size === 'xsm' ? 2.5 : 2} />,
  },
});
```

Keep the function pure and synchronous: it computes its result from the request only, with no hooks, DOM or SVG measurement, browser globals, network access, mutable module state, or side effects. Its result is final. Returning `null` draws no glyph instead of falling back to a registered or built-in icon, while a `null` or `undefined` node entry still falls back. A child theme that sets an entry for a key replaces the inherited entry for that key.

A theme lists the icon variant names it supports once, with `iconVariants` in `defineTheme()`. The list belongs to the whole theme, never to one icon, and Astryx defines no names of its own. Inside `defineTheme`, an entry function receives `variant` typed as the listed names, or `undefined`, so a misspelled name is a type error. An entry that receives `undefined` draws its normal artwork.

```
import {defineTheme} from '@astryxdesign/core/theme';
import {InfoFilledIcon, InfoIcon} from './brand-icons';

export const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  iconVariants: ['outline', 'filled'],
  icons: {
    info: ({size, variant}) =>
      variant === 'filled' ? <InfoFilledIcon /> : <InfoIcon bold={size === 'sm'} />,
  },
});

brandTheme.iconVariants; // ['outline', 'filled']
```

A theme that `extends` another inherits its list unless it sets its own; a list it sets replaces the inherited one instead of merging with it.

Request a variant with `Icon`'s `variant` prop. The entry receives it only when the active theme lists it; an entry the theme inherited also needs the theme that defined it to list it. Otherwise the entry receives `undefined` and draws its normal artwork, so an unlisted variant never breaks rendering. A `registerIcons()` entry receives the variants the active theme lists, and a node entry draws the same node for every variant. An icon component passed directly to `Icon` never receives `variant`, and `variant` is never written to the DOM: it selects artwork, so there is no theming selector for it.

```
<Icon icon="info" variant="filled" />  // brandTheme: the filled artwork
<Icon icon="info" />                    // the normal artwork
```

An application declares the variant names it uses by augmenting `IconVariantMap` from `@astryxdesign/core/Icon`. `IconVariant` is those names, and an entry passed to `registerIcons()` receives `variant` typed as `IconVariant`. `astryx theme build` writes this augmentation for a built theme's list.

```
declare module '@astryxdesign/core/Icon' {
  interface IconVariantMap {
    outline: true;
    filled: true;
  }
}
```

A glyph that belongs to one component or library gets a namespaced key (`numberInput:stepperDown`, `richtext:bold`) instead of a new built-in meaning. It resolves through the same registry and a theme overrides it the same way, but the shared IconName list stays reserved for glyphs the whole system uses, so adding one does not make every downstream theme's icons grow a key.

```
// The component renders it like any other name, keeping size and color.
<Icon icon="numberInput:stepperDown" size="xsm" />

// A theme maps it independently of the shared chevron.
export const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  icons: {
    chevronDown: <ChevronDownIcon />,
    'numberInput:stepperDown': <CaretDownFilledIcon />,
  },
});
```

Outside core, pass a fallback to `getExtendedIcon(key, fallback)` so the glyph renders with no theme.

A component icon slot names why an icon appears in one component, such as `code-block-copy` for CodeBlock's copy button. `defineTheme({componentIcons})` maps a slot to one of the shared icon names. A theme can then change the icon one component uses without changing that name's artwork everywhere; `icons` still chooses the artwork for each name. Each component's docs list its slots, their state, and their fallbacks (`astryx component CodeBlock`).

```
export const brandTheme = defineTheme({
  name: 'brand',
  componentIcons: {
    'code-block-copy': ({copied}) => ({icon: copied ? 'checkDouble' : 'copy'}),
  },
});
```

A slot value is a shared icon name, `null` to hide the icon, or a partial request of `icon`, `variant`, and `size`. A field the request leaves out keeps the component's own value. A slot with state also accepts a pure, synchronous function of that state that returns a request: returning `null` hides the icon, and returning `undefined` changes nothing. A request `size` changes only the icon, never the control around it. Icon content a component's caller passes wins over the slot.

An unknown icon name, a variant the theme does not list, or a size other than `xsm`, `sm`, `md`, or `lg` is ignored for that field, with one development warning. A theme that extends another merges `componentIcons` by slot ({@link generic:author-a-theme}). `theme.componentIcons` reads back names, `null` values, and static requests, and leaves out the slots a function resolves.

If your audience includes Tier 2 browsers and you need correct layer positioning, you have three options, cheapest first. All of them are decisions you make for your audience; Astryx does not impose one.

- Polyfill anchor positioning. Load a CSS anchor positioning polyfill conditionally behind an `@supports` check so it costs nothing on Tier 1. This is the lowest-effort way to get correct positioning; polyfills may not cover every position-fallback feature.
- Provide a JS positioning fallback. Detect missing support with `CSS.supports("anchor-name", "--x")` and position layers with a measurement-based library (e.g. a floating-element positioner) when it returns false. More code, full control.
- Accept degraded positioning. Document for your users that on Tier 2 browsers, layered surfaces open correctly but may not sit next to their trigger. Cheapest, and often fine for internal tools on evergreen browsers.

Feature-detect at runtime rather than sniffing user agents:

```
// Popover API (Tier 2 and up)
const hasPopover = typeof HTMLElement !== 'undefined'
  && typeof HTMLElement.prototype.showPopover === 'function';

// CSS anchor positioning (Tier 1)
const hasAnchorPositioning = CSS.supports('anchor-name', '--x');

// CSS light-dark() (Tier 2 and up)
const hasLightDark = CSS.supports('color', 'light-dark(#000, #fff)');
```
